# unbounded-rpc-call allowlist (T-2669) — consumed by scripts/check-unbounded-rpc-call.sh
#
# One call site per line: `<relpath>::<enclosing-fn>::unbounded-rpc-call  # <reason>`.
#
# A listed site is still COUNTED AND REPORTED — it never vanishes from the scan total —
# it simply does not fire. There are TWO classes below and they mean opposite things.
#
# ── CLASS 1: INTENTIONAL long-poll (a permanent, justified acknowledgement) ──
# The RPC's contract is to block until data arrives or the SERVER-side timeout fires,
# with the bound passed as a `timeout_ms` RPC param. A naive client bound shorter than
# the server's would tear down a healthy wait — the bound genuinely belongs server-side.
# These are classified by the RPC METHOD the fn dispatches, not by its name.
#
# ── CLASS 2: NOT YET MIGRATED (a ledger of an open question) ──
# These are the T-2641 hang class, still live. They are acknowledged so this check can
# be a RATCHET from day one — a NEW unbounded call site is in neither set and fires
# immediately — WITHOUT alarming daily on a 156-site backlog the human has not yet
# scheduled. This is NOT a claim that they are safe. Each one can still hang forever.
#
# WHY THEY ARE NOT ALREADY FIXED. T-2669's own Decisions section rejected an autonomous
# batch migration: a client timeout returns an ERROR where callers and tests previously
# saw a block, so the per-verb timeout value is a design judgment (a `ping` wants ~10s;
# `exec` / `interact` / `agent.ask` drive a session command and may legitimately run far
# longer). That judgment is the human decision this ledger is waiting on. Removing a line
# re-fires that site.
#
# MEASUREMENT NOTE. T-2669's body states 36 functions. Measured on 2026-08-28 with this
# check, BEFORE any migration: 188 call sites scanned, 161 unbounded. The filed figure was
# a material undercount — the analytics tool family in tools.rs grew substantially after
# the task was filed on 2026-08-12, and every one of those handlers took the unbounded
# form. The remediation is ~4.5x its filed estimate, which is why the per-verb timeout
# decision T-2669 routed to a human matters more now, not less.
#
# The 5 fast-read sites whose timeout value needs no judgment (termlink_ping,
# termlink_status, termlink_hub_governor_status, termlink_channel_list,
# termlink_channel_cv_keys — all short reads where a 30s bound is unambiguously right)
# are migrated, and the tree measures 183 scanned / 156 unbounded. Those 156 are the
# lines below: 16 CLASS 1, 140 CLASS 2.
#
# RECORD CORRECTION (T-2669, 2026-08-30). The paragraph above previously read "After
# this session migrated the 5 fast-read sites ..." and was NOT true when written. The
# migration was done on the charter-review worktree; commit 6b587e826 carried this
# ledger and scripts/check-unbounded-rpc-call.sh across to main but touched no .rs file
# at all, so the 5 sites stayed on the raw unbounded form and the check FIRED on exactly
# them. The ledger asserted a code state that did not exist — and its 183/156 figures
# were a forecast of the migrated tree, not a measurement of this one.
#
# That is the failure this ledger is otherwise built to prevent, occurring one layer up:
# a ratchet's baseline is only meaningful if it describes the tree it ships with, and a
# baseline written ahead of the code cannot be told apart from one written behind it.
# The numbers were right, which is what made it hard to see — the check fired, the
# header explained the fire away, and the two disagreed silently. T-2669 landed the
# actual migration (client::rpc_call_with_timeout, the missing &Path-based bounded
# helper that AC#1 named, plus the 5 call sites); 183/156 is now measured, not forecast.
#

